h point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=0 \\\\ -2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+3 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-4{sin}^{2}⁡t+3 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,-2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the points $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the points $(2,0)$ and $(-2,0).$" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446473 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446487 1069 "q-83437" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446487 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.$ You are given that the function has no critical points." 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446487 1072 #uuid "a2f4f1da-3ab7-5268-ad0e-cfd1ed45e614" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446487 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nSince f has no critical points in D , the list of candidates for the extrema won't contain critical points.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\le 1$, this equation gives the solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval,  $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain candidate points: \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(0,1).$" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446467 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446488 1069 "q-86706" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446488 1071 "![](images/9c/9ce1b06ac9fc3caeed0a1f7866511be42b4c127a0bdddfd40b74e211ed24e02a.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\},$ shown above." 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446488 1072 #uuid "e240afca-8d54-5fc1-a704-b3948ecfdb13" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446488 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2},\\,4xy\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4xy=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=R \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the entire segment of solutions $\\{(x,0)\\,:\\,x\\in [0,2]\\}.$ Since all these points do not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include them in the list of candidates for extrema (they lie on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider them in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =2(t)(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =2(0)(t)^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The quarter-circle connecting $(0,2)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2(t)(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{2} \\\\  & =8t-2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (8t-2{t}^{3})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 8-6{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2(4-3{t}^{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution, $t=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ inside the interval $t\\in (0,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one  candidate point, $(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $\\frac{32\\sqrt{3}}{9}$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the points on the line segment connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0),$ and on the line segment connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $\\frac{32\\sqrt{3}}{9}$ at the point $(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}).$" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446472 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446489 1069 "q-83438" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446489 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=3xy$ on the closed elliptical region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}\\le 2\\}.$" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446489 1072 #uuid "e78c82da-b48f-5dab-aeb6-fcde924129f1" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446489 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo do that, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3y,\\,3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}3y=0 \\\\ 3x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{2},0),\\,(1,\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(-1,\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(-1,-\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(1,-\\frac{1}{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(1,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(-1,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(-1,-\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(1,-\\frac{1}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the points $(-1,\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(1,-\\frac{1}{3}),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(1,\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(-1,-\\frac{1}{3}).$" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446478 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446490 1069 "q-37555" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446490 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=(x-2)^{2}+y$ on the closed region \n$D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x\\}.$ \nYou are given  that $f$ has no critical points in the region and on the boundary of the region,  the points that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $(0,0)$, $(2,4),$ and  $(1,1).$" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446490 1072 #uuid "f2504620-1607-5645-b4d2-e55a27da4e3f" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true] [17592186446490 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nNotice that we are told that $f$ has no critical points in the region.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,4),\\,(1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =(0-2)^{2}+0 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(2,4) & =(2-2)^{2}+(4) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(1,1) & =(1-2)^{2}+(1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2$ at the point $(1,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $4$ at the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,4).$" 13194139534976 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-001a4990-bc6c-5661-a5d2-d58cf6282115" 17592186446402 "answer-0461d323-c426-57d7-8a31-96c6ccb0b392" 17592186446403 "answer-0e3e8dda-0412-5eec-8ede-228822513c7f" 17592186446404 "answer-0f08487e-af26-5c1f-962f-ed237732c2fd" 17592186446405 "answer-0f5adf40-0426-5a10-8817-dc97f008412e" 17592186446406 "answer-147e44af-3fa3-5f20-8f78-baf96a687c49" 17592186446407 "answer-180b8d8a-e5ba-53e7-85fe-0018dfc11e0e" 17592186446408 "answer-1b49ff3f-4e51-53f3-a015-1753de86c110" 17592186446409 "answer-1d1b623e-7729-5df0-a681-2ee123f5e5bc" 17592186446410 "answer-2fcbaf68-d293-51cc-a576-3b40121223bf" 17592186446411 "answer-323833f4-28f9-5c1a-bbca-309065f42ade" 17592186446412 "answer-3c23c898-f1a2-5f4e-b367-26b24d6dcd88" 17592186446413 "answer-3d8beee5-d16b-5ad8-81c1-9b0ed36361cf" 17592186446414 "answer-4051cf24-382c-5bd2-aff3-583e10956692" 17592186446415 "a